courses.learning_outcomes:
The course will:
- deepen students’ knowledge and critical understanding of the complex relationship between language policy, culture, and identity in a diverse range of socio-political contexts in Asia (e.g., Hong Kong, Malaysia, Singapore, Japan, India, Brunei Darussalam, North and South Korea, Pakistan, Vietnam, Cambodia, China, Nepal);
- enhance their understanding of the impact of globalization, glocalization, and internationalization on language policies, local cultures, and identities;
- enhance their competence and confidence in both written and oral English;
- provide the opportunity for students to work collaboratively in small groups to investigate language planning and policy issues in an Asian setting of their choice;
- require groups to produce a coherent, substantive written report and oral presentation based on their research findings;
- help students polish their professional presentation skills in a formal conference setting.

By the end of the course, students will be able to:

• recognize and identify the concept of utopia in science fiction writing;
• discriminate between the various representations of utopia in science fiction literature;
• analyze the ways in which the notion of utopia has been drawn on by writers to critique contemporary society;
• draw on the theoretical and critical material discussed in this course in order to produce both a formal essay and an original short story, which have as their focus the concept of utopia.

After this course, students should be better able to:
• discuss the nature and scope of intercultural communication theory and practice;
• identify the role of intercultural communication in the wider context of applied linguistics;
• explain and use related key concepts and terms appropriately;
• assess theories and key research findings in intercultural communication;
• read primary sources critically and effectively;
• describe and assess research techniques used in intercultural communication research;
• describe the nature of cultural differences and assess their impact on linguistic and nonverbal communication in intercultural interactions in Hong Kong and elsewhere;
• work individually (and collaboratively) to produce oral and written work that is clear, concise, and coherent.

Upon completion of this course, students will be able to:
• understand the key contexts and Issues in ESP, and how they are related to developments in English language teaching, and more broadly, to applied linguistics research;
• review current research, exploring functional description of variation in academic and professional discourses and genres, and use insights from analysis as input to the development of ESP materials;
• identify analytical insights from current research in discourse and genre studies relevant to the development, design and teaching of English for academic and professional communication programmes; and,
• analyse specific instances of academic and professional ESP course materials currently in use, particularly focusing on authenticity in the selection of input, opportunities available for exploiting new technology and digital media, and above-all, the use of appropriate ESP methodology in teaching, learning and evaluating interdiscursive performance in specialised contexts.

1) By the end of this course, students should be able to:

- understand the role of language in human affairs;
- apply knowledge to solve language-related problems in specific situations in which people use and learn languages;
- make connections between fields, theories, and research methods in applied linguistics; and
- carry out research designed to produce practical applications, to generate further knowledge, or to validate theories.

2) For students in MA English (Applied English Linguistics) or MPhil English (Applied English Linguistics)

After taking the course, students should:

- Be familiar with the International Phonetic Alphabet (IPA) and its usage in teaching and conducting research

- Understand the main issues involved in the acquisition of an L2 phonology, including linguistics and social factors;
- Be aware of research on training and classroom practices as well as research on various linguistic and social factors;
- Be able to analyze the pronunciation phenomena of learners and apply phonological acquisition theories to explain the phenomena;
- Be able to apply teaching theories and acquisition theories to develop pronunciation teaching plans and materials for learners.

By the end of this course, students are expected to be able to...

* Explain the underlying cognitive processes involved in reading in English as a second language;
* Review research findings on the second language reading from a critical perspective;
* Analyze existing English reading instructional materials (graded readers, reading comprehension tasks, lesson plans, and syllabus) based on theories of second language reading; and
* Construct effective second language reading teaching materials and syllabus.
